no man's sky is an exploration games set  in a vast galaxy of over 18 quintillion  planets this is a game about travel  survival and commerce backed by  impressive tech that allows for near  seamless transitions from ground to  space  every facet contains multiple layers to  consider while some details will make  your journey feel more genuine than most  there are flaws that occasionally get in  the way of your investment in the  Odyssey however there's an intriguing  narrative that puts new context on your  in-game actions making for a fascinating  experience that ultimately trumps issues  that appear early on  most of the things you see and interact  with during your journey have been  arranged by an algorithm the sheer  number of variation of world and  inhabiting species is too large to fully  comprehend but because the variety is  defined by a computer pulling from a  restricted pool of options animals  appear more like slapdash creations than  thoughtful constructions you will spend  hours whittling away at rocks plants and  asteroids in search of commodities using  your multi-tool on the ground or your  ship's weapon in space as you explore  you have to monitor your exosuit  equipment to maintain protection from  hazardous conditions and occasionally to  recover from violent encounters combat  is a secondary activity however but it  occurs often enough to make the games  unrefined multi-tool controls a standout  issue aiming the weapon component of  your multi-tool is finicky enough to  make these encounters more of an  annoyance than an enjoyable challenge  battles with space pirates lack  excitement in-depth offering simple  visual targets that you fire into as  enemies storm from afar and little else  to consider  your starship and exosuit have a limited  number of slots that can hold stacks of  resources or we use to apply equipment  upgrades so you try to be efficient and  work with what you have but no man's sky  doesn't make this easy you have to  navigate a plane grid of items using a  slow moving cursor holding down a button  for seconds at a time to confirm every  action managing your inventory is a  large part of no man's sky so this  becomes a regular annoyance in space  stations and outpost strewn across  planets even counter friendly NPCs that  are open to trade at first these  encounters offer temporary relief from  prolonged isolation but interactions are  largely devoid of meaning at best you  can learn bits of each species lexicon  by discovering translation monoliths but  even this process lacks substantial  meaning as translations reveal truly  stiff and impersonal dialogue the  starships you find come in a range of  models with varying color palettes and  accessories and while you may get lucky  and find a wrecked but improved ship to  repair and call your own ships are  readily available in Space Station  hangars where traders come and go in  real time in a galaxy with no real  friends or social ties it's easy to look  at possessions as a way to curb  loneliness and provide meaning to your  journey as time goes on however you may  lose the high that comes from your new  purchase and seek another a cycle that  continues and grows increasingly  draining if you adhere to the direct  path towards the center of the galaxy in  pursuit of the game's primary goal no  man's sky grows tiresome in less time  than a game with this much potential  ought to no matter how many solar  systems you jump to or planets you  explore you will likely grow tired of  repetitive NPC interactions and planets  implied but shallow variety you'll also  lose interest in new ships perhaps the  journey altogether however tucked neatly  into the galaxy is a narrative path  delivered so subtly that you may miss  the fact that the first decision you  make in the game activating a distress  beacon connects you to a mysterious  force known as Atlas as you continue to  travel the Stars you encounter  individuals that will steer you towards  Atlas stations fast temple-like spaces  with altars that allow you to convene  with the spirit of Atlas at first what  Atlas is can be difficult to pin down  but if you continue to heat its call it  will open your eyes to the greater  truths about you your journey and the  galaxy the realizations that Atlas stirs  in your character's mind address big  picture  questions pertaining to not only the  game but life itself it forced me to  confront the point of my wandering the  value of material wealth and the reason  for existence atlas in many ways  illustrates the value religion plays in  some people's lives but it also quite  cleverly examines the role a game like  no man's sky plays in our personal  fulfillment when I was on the brink of  disillusionment Atlas renewed my spirit  and fueled my desire to press onward in  search of a greater meaning  no man's sky is immediately a massive  game and it will entertain the inter  collector in you for a while the more  you get to know it the more you  recognize its faults but if you consider  everything it has to offer no man's sky  becomes more than a collection of  slightly different worlds at a seemingly  never-ending galaxy it becomes an  examination of the meaning of life in a  way that's more valuable than all the  gold or starships in the galaxy
 

